Fix the AX88179A TX wedge on frames that never fit a TX buffer

The 179A path advertised tso_max_size = tx_casecade_size, but each frame
needs an 8-byte header inside that buffer. GSO frames of casecade-7 ..
casecade-1 bytes (23545..23551) passed the core's skb->len >= gso_max_size
check, never fit, and were requeued at the queue head forever; a USB reset
keeps the queue, so TX stayed wedged until the netdev was recreated.

Advertise casecade minus the header, drop (with a warning) any frame too
large even for an empty buffer in both TX fixups, never submit an empty
TX URB, and clamp the TX NAPI poll's return value to its budget.

diff --git a/src/ax88179_178a.c b/src/ax88179_178a.c
index 7bc1727..efc6a6a 100644
--- a/src/ax88179_178a.c
+++ b/src/ax88179_178a.c
@@ -1082,6 +1082,21 @@ static int ax88179_tx_fixup(struct ax_device *axdev, struct tx_desc *desc)
 			break;
 
 		if ((skb->len + AX_TX_HEADER_LEN) > remain) {
+			if (tx_data == desc->head) {
+				/*
+				 * Too large even for an empty buffer.  Requeueing
+				 * it would block the queue head forever (a USB
+				 * reset keeps the queue), so drop it.
+				 */
+				if (net_ratelimit())
+					netdev_warn(axdev->netdev,
+						    "dropping oversized TX frame: len %u gso_size %u\n",
+						    skb->len,
+						    skb_shinfo(skb)->gso_size);
+				stats->tx_dropped += skb_shinfo(skb)->gso_segs ?: 1;
+				dev_kfree_skb_any(skb);
+				continue;
+			}
 			__skb_queue_head(&skb_head, skb);
 			break;
 		}
diff --git a/src/ax88179a_772d.c b/src/ax88179a_772d.c
index f751e79..2449e58 100644
--- a/src/ax88179a_772d.c
